9693d6816cf527c19" :answer/id #uuid "870c42bc-68b1-5949-82e0-ca5ac728af0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181970/field-2/e4e9dbe71f63d8d81739fdcbf2df64ccccd4c8f5d299ddc4b1c1f96031adb38e" :answer/id #uuid "1f161290-f298-5050-a6c6-369c17edd8e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181970/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "55fe9a80-4cbe-5c87-9157-68917a7de008" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-181970/field-2/cc57b3a2811193b1201e1d8d139011f0b8b63f9cad9aaf57f40ab2b8d40ee2eb" :answer-field/choices ["q-181970/field-2/cc57b3a2811193b1201e1d8d139011f0b8b63f9cad9aaf57f40ab2b8d40ee2eb" "q-181970/field-2/22814de28a0b1b4f20d5edd62707d192b78050d457a70349795d30d86dfcd7fa" "q-181970/field-2/55f4eef1ef55065220056c9644bf18d21761bc8db2d8f5c41f767a2fc9186b70" "q-181970/field-2/7d149b60a8e2a3be02476cb4e4d26616193c2006082edf79693d6816cf527c19" "q-181970/field-2/e4e9dbe71f63d8d81739fdcbf2df64ccccd4c8f5d299ddc4b1c1f96031adb38e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181970" :question/id #uuid "d0486dc3-5d9e-5f68-9cf5-938a2bd521f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-181970" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/010d4befaa2bd2142cf63010f79f071df331bb6394eb65e4f883752cb04a2cb4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x+1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x+1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -x+1}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-181970/field-1" "q-181970/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181970"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186195441 :answer-field/correct 17592186195444 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186195448 :answer-field/correct 17592186195450 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181983"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/cce16725dddcfc83098ee2323576b8e01bbc3f2625d17cb7e6030826ad9fbcd8.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,x-1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny<x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x-1}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-181988/field-1/203f88d94a38bb859e78e84aea518cf34952815758cf391887040455367d62ef" :answer/id #uuid "4a52fd96-abf5-5c8d-aef8-bbd2ecb65bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "N" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181988/field-1/07d4d169afe54b72e6508fedd544e5d357fac